## Rules

- Write short and to the point narrative instead of bullet points or enumerations, use them only when the content requires it (e.g. listing steps)
- Make any answer concise. Sacrifice grammar for the sake of concision.

## Coding

- When writing or reviewing code, follow the YAGNI principle and prefer one-line solutions when they stay readable.
- When a file is to large over 600 lines, it must be split
- Do not implement large functions that passes 100 lines or reach 4 nested levels

## Plans

- Use diagrams, charts, and code samples.
- Explain files to be touch.
- Explain the package structure if the change is large

## When spawning subagents

- Use gpt-6-luna at high effort for documentation lookup, codebase mapping, extraction, classification, and simple audits. Raise to xhigh only when the task requires judgment beyond a clear rubric.
- Use gpt-6-sol at medium effort for isolated implementation with clear acceptance criteria, multi-file changes, debugging, or non-obvious test failures.
- Use gpt-6-sol at high effort for security, concurrency, migrations, architecture, and ambiguous high-value work. Use high effort when an incorrect result is costly or an independent review is required.
- Never use Max or Ultra thinking level.
- Treat Sol as the highest permitted model unless the user explicitly asks to use Astra.
